# Engineering context and tool routing

| Tool | Role |
|------|------|
| **GBrain** | Durable project knowledge, history, previous decisions |
| **Graphify** | Structural codebase understanding and change-impact analysis |
| **Superpowers** | Engineering method: planning, TDD, debugging, verification |
| **gstack** | Selective additional review / QA when complexity or risk justifies it |

## When to use each tool

**GBrain** — prior decisions, historical constraints, conventions not in code. Not for source-code search.

**Graphify** — architecture discovery, dependencies, change impact. Run `graphify query` before broad grep on structural questions. Graph is per-repo at `graphify-out/`.

**Superpowers** — process discipline (`writing-plans`, `test-driven-development`, `systematic-debugging`, `verification-before-completion`). Do not wrap or replace its bootstrap.

**gstack** — selective review/QA (`/gstack-review`, `/gstack-qa`, `/gstack-investigate`). Not mandatory per slice.

## Avoid tool inflation

Do not chain all four tools for every small change.

- Small bug: Superpowers debugging + targeted tests may suffice.
- Architecture change: GBrain + Graphify + Superpowers planning + optional gstack review.
- Historical question: GBrain alone may suffice.
- Dependency question: Graphify alone may suffice.

## Rust

When creating, modifying, reviewing, or refactoring Rust, follow `rust-engineering-standard.mdc` (plugin rule; attaches on `*.rs` / Cargo files). Correctness, memory/concurrency safety, and explicit invariants come before cleverness or hypothetical performance. Do not use `unwrap()`/`expect()` for expected runtime failure.

HTTP/API with Axum, Tokio, and Tower additionally follows `rust-axum-engineering-standard.mdc`. Handlers are transport adapters: extract, invoke one application operation, map the result. Domain types must not return HTTP status codes.

Persistence with SQLx and PostgreSQL follows `rust-sqlx-engineering-standard.mdc`. SQL is architecture, not an implementation detail. Domain types must not depend on `PgPool` / `Transaction`.

Testing and quality follow `testing-quality-engineering-standard.mdc`. Test behavior and invariants, not coverage percentage. Use the lowest layer that reliably proves the behavior.

Security and supply chain follow `security-supply-chain-engineering-standard.mdc`. Least privilege, fail closed; secrets must not be logged or committed.

## Engineering audits

Repository-wide or scoped engineering audits use plugin skills under `skills/engineering/`. Start with `/rust-engineering` (default **audit**). **Fix** is allowed only against a completed open audit in `docs/engineering-audits/rust/`; never invent findings or silently re-audit in fix mode. Further domains (Axum, SQLx, testing, security, full audit) come later.

The audit skill reads `references/rust-engineering-standard.md`, which must stay in sync with `rules/rust-engineering-standard.mdc` (same body; the `.mdc` keeps Cursor globs).

Project-specific rules in `AGENTS.md` take precedence over this global routing.
